de","maturity":"draft","page_type":"entity","path":"pages/entities/notasithlord-peerd-cac23452b4de.md","record_type":"page","review_state":"mechanically_checked","schema_version":"1.0","source_ids":[],"title":"notasithlord/peerd","updated_at":"2026-09-14T02:23:22Z","updated_by_operation":"op_23953f901a384964a25ec63d753351ac"}
{"access":"public","aliases":[],"assertions":[{"claim_ids":["clm_47bd4b11d4833074141f3c11470f7d7ee0fefd90aa9243b3c9b4b295b36dedbb"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Credentials, network rules, confirmations, and audit stay with the extension; egress controls include refusal of private-network/cloud-metadata targets, denylist checks, and redirect blocking, with per-operation scoped network policies."},{"claim_ids":["clm_6199c440af142e87948f652ed539e828ec4ee6ef42b59e5d5ae9f8a003ae860a"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: development uses Bun commands (bun install, gen:dev, test, typecheck, lint, e2e:verify, preflight) across three test surfaces (Bun unit tests, in-browser Chrome/Gecko tests, live Chrome E2E), plus a red-team suite; generated files like extension/manifest.json must not be hand-edited and CI checks them for drift."},{"claim_ids":["clm_7ffb39d9dd53d3709f9ddb054d15da4cc24521bcb49841076596aff7ecbb9ea6"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The extension has five main modules, each exposing its public API via index.js: peerd-provider (model adapters), peerd-egress (vault, network policy, denylist, audit), peerd-engine (WebVM/Notebook/App/headless execution), peerd-runtime (agent loop, actors, tools, sessions, memory, permissions), and peerd-distributed (preview-only P2P)."},{"claim_ids":["clm_804ed06809a60749efeded84ac6a12179a8bf6c2a03d12e4c41016c38d8f8492"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Sessions, memory, skills, goals, review, and checkpoints live in the extension; a local vault holds secrets and protected security records, with passphrase unlock always available and passkey unlock dependent on WebAuthn PRF support."},{"claim_ids":["clm_911ac392ddb6c1f71ea69f84caed15ac36739f8f5b786f40565c75f572a8bb36"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The source extension is vanilla JavaScript with ES modules running directly when loaded unpacked, with no development bundler or transpiler; release packaging with Bun minifies only a disposable staging copy and preserves module boundaries and vendored bytes."},{"claim_ids":["clm_9d436bff6373fd6860f6912f796530a39ac58b9820ec5f48b44e647f25d866eb"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The shipped extension has no npm runtime dependencies; third-party runtime code is vendored under extension/vendor/ with SOURCE.txt provenance files and SHA-256 pinning in vendor.lock.json, verified by bun run check:vendor."},{"claim_ids":["clm_b0e0b7e93437fe7741ab03b85ed11bba215278617b02b76b74ea6b49c13ec04b"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"peerd is described as a general-purpose agent runtime built on browser primitives (Workers, origins, sandboxing, OPFS, WASM/WASI, WebRTC, WebAuthn, WebExtensions) that runs inside Chrome and Firefox."},{"claim_ids":["clm_daf7653b96fb12bd81fdb2e05601b573c5f9449df6538c91823d10b66c9978e0"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The main agent delegates page and compute-environment work to separate keyless actors whose tools are scoped to that environment; non-orchestrator agent loops run in dedicated worker heaps, and requests fail if the browser cannot prove that boundary."}],"claim_ids":["clm_47bd4b11d4833074141f3c11470f7d7ee0fefd90aa9243b3c9b4b295b36dedbb","clm_6199c440af142e87948f652ed539e828ec4ee6ef42b59e5d5ae9f8a003ae860a","clm_7ffb39d9dd53d3709f9ddb054d15da4cc24521bcb49841076596aff7ecbb9ea6","clm_804ed06809a60749efeded84ac6a12179a8bf6c2a03d12e4c41016c38d8f8492","clm_911ac392ddb6c1f71ea69f84caed15ac36739f8f5b786f40565c75f572a8bb36","clm_9d436bff6373fd6860f6912f796530a39ac58b9820ec5f48b44e647f25d866eb","clm_b0e0b7e93437fe7741ab03b85ed11bba215278617b02b76b74ea6b49c13ec04b","clm_daf7653b96fb12bd81fdb2e05601b573c5f9449df6538c91823d10b66c9978e0"],"created_at":"2026-09-14T02:23:22Z","created_by_operation":"op_23953f901a384964a25ec63d753351ac","id":"pg_ae8456091ded510686d2e1b00dfdfccd","maturity":"draft","page_type":"source","path":"pages/sources/notasithlord-peerd-readme-md-12d1a54976bc-e1b00dfdfccd.md","record_type":"page","review_state":"mechanically_checked","schema_version":"1.0","source_ids":["src_fff4f1b8fce156b9ae5b28baa4870253"],"title":"NotASithLord/peerd/README.md @ 12d1a54976bc","updated_at":"2026-09-14T02:23:22Z","updated_by_operation":"op_23953f901a384964a25ec63d753351ac"}
{"access":"public","aliases":[],"assertions":[{"claim_ids":["clm_2d5ed33ed3326fa1f189374f1712af495251c40c0e4a91394e3be81d744ba76c"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"App manifests use peerd.json with fields like kind, entry, agent (kind bound-app, profile, surface, name, instructions, runtime), and capabilities; a conforming Git repository places peerd.json and its entry file at the root."},{"claim_ids":["clm_493a5940a94099b8603b9a0fef44d07ade0064dd0ce5b00f2f566449ab05e48f"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Apps integrate via window.peerd APIs: agent.open() reveals a trusted drawer only during real user activation, agent.expose() declares observe/act semantic adapters, and peerd.data provides bounded JSON persistence (1 MB per write) mapped to data/<key>.json in the App's working tree."}],"claim_ids":["clm_2d5ed33ed3326fa1f189374f1712af495251c40c0e4a91394e3be81d744ba76c","clm_493a5940a94099b8603b9a0fef44d07ade0064dd0ce5b00f2f566449ab05e48f"],"created_at":"2026-09-14T02:23:22Z","created_by_operation":"op_23953f901a384964a25ec63d753351ac","id":"pg_b805b05bc3955ff08ca0dc77505e538a","maturity":"draft","page_type":"source","path":"pages/sources/notasithlord-peerd-docs-app-actors-md-12d1a54976bc-dc77505e538a.md","record_type":"page","review_state":"mechanically_checked","schema_version":"1.0","source_ids":["src_03743a43d00a55f9bfaae00e25cbd01e"],"title":"NotASithLord/peerd/docs/APP-ACTORS.md @ 12d1a54976bc","updated_at":"2026-09-14T02:23:22Z","updated_by_operation":"op_23953f901a384964a25ec63d753351ac"}
{"access":"public","aliases":[],"assertions":[{"claim_ids":["clm_49c4ba9d289e2de2df1495b5d9705982d3b48fdc34cbca22dd730db9c4c20f70"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"On Firefox, WebVM cannot boot from an extension page, threaded WebAssembly is unavailable, the Pod 'js' command is refused, Apps are disabled pending Firefox 154 adoption, offscreen-hosted tools are absent or degraded, and CDP-only automation features fall back to chrome.scripting."}],"claim_ids":["clm_49c4ba9d289e2de2df1495b5d9705982d3b48fdc34cbca22dd730db9c4c20f70"],"created_at":"2026-09-14T02:23:22Z","created_by_operation":"op_23953f901a384964a25ec63d753351ac","id":"pg_ba5d74c2697f5cef863353ab027ff3b4","maturity":"draft","page_type":"source","path":"pages/sources/notasithlord-peerd-docs-browser-compatibility-md-12d1a54976bc-53ab027ff3b4.md","record_type":"page","review_state":"mechanically_checked","schema_version":"1.0","source_ids":["src_117164f539cc537ca3e038469252f654"],"title":"NotASithLord/peerd/docs/BROWSER-COMPATIBILITY.md @ 12d1a54976bc","updated_at":"2026-09-14T02:23:22Z","updated_by_operation":"op_23953f901a384964a25ec63d753351ac"}
